  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/... Sunday continues to \nlook like one fine day across Southeast, with the potential for \nsome fog development. Stay tuned. Mariners moving around the inner\nchannels should be aware of outflow winds, especially overnight \ninto Monday as high pressure builds across the Yukon and BC \nprovinces. One impact from outflow will be cold air once again \ninfiltrating the Panhandle, especially those closest to the Coast \nRange and major river inlets, priming us for snow. Expect a large \nsystem to move in off the Bering Monday steering plenty of \nmoisture into our area Tuesday into Wednesday. Snow impacts will \nbe directly tied to how prolific the outflow event is for the \nstart of next week, the resulting cold air infiltration, and how \nquickly relatively warmer southerlies can scrub out the cold \nairmass at the surface. For now, the published forecast reflects \nminor snow for some communities separate from Haines and Skagway. \nIt certainly feels like the first snow event for us humble few in \nSoutheast; however, accumulating snow (outside of the Haines and \nKlondike highways) looks modest, likely only some flakes to \nperhaps an inch before turning to rain. Yakutat will also see some\nsnow Tuesday. Stay tuned as we monitor for developments thru the \nweekend. \n\nConfidence is high on cold air moving in at the surface Monday, \nalong with moisture timing Tuesday and Wednesday. Largest \nuncertainty is how warm will the incoming airmass be and how \nquickly we can scrub out any persistent cold air at the surface. \nWhile it looks like a decent overrunning setup, the limiting \nfactor is the duration of the Monday outflow, limiting snow \npotential.",
